# v310.1 — TENANT INTEGRITY TERMINAL NORMALIZATION
#
# Four tenant-scoped surfaces stop defaulting to a tenant UUID pinned at
# migration time and derive from public.current_tenant_id() instead:
# proposal_versions, photo_library, publication_themes and blueprints.
# proposal_versions rows whose tenancy contradicts their parent proposal are
# corrected from the parent, which is the authoritative owner of that
# relationship.
#
# Successor migration: four ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS tenant_id
# uuid REFERENCES tenants(id), four ALTER COLUMN ... SET DEFAULT, one UPDATE
# bounded by a parent join, and the marker function. No table, index, trigger,
# policy, grant or type is created, dropped or altered. No authority, ceremony,
# projection or availability object from v306 through v310 is touched. No tenant
# is created, renamed or removed. No data is purged. No tenancy abstraction,
# helper function or provisioning machinery is introduced.
#
# PREDECESSOR-TOLERANT. Two shapes are accepted and converge identically:
# the column present carrying the literal-default defect, and the column absent
# because the guarded historical block never fired. The ADD COLUMN clause is the
# only thing that differs between them, and it is a no-op on the first.
#
# DATA CHANGE — DECLARED. This release writes to existing rows of
# public.proposal_versions only, setting tenant_id to the parent proposal's
# tenant_id where the two disagree and the parent declares a tenancy. In
# EventCore production that is exactly five rows. No row is inserted and no row
# is deleted; one-shot UT-5 asserts the pre and post row counts are equal.
# photo_library, publication_themes and blueprints receive NO data repair —
# they have no governing parent relation, so any reassignment would be
# invention. Production currently holds zero rows in all three.
#
# BLUEPRINTS DRIFT. Production already reads current_tenant_id() on
# blueprints.tenant_id although the frozen chain can still produce the old
# literal default there. Applied out of band, recorded in no release.
# Classified PRE-EXISTING UNDOCUMENTED PRODUCTION DRIFT — CAPABILITY NOW
# ABSORBED BY v310.1. Production is not reversed to match history, no separate
# repair release is created, and no historical release evidence is rewritten.
#
# BOOKING CRM IS NOT CONTACTED. Shared Cross-Product Ledger X-013: SHARED CORE,
# common ancestor, EventCore repairs independently, Booking CRM already repaired
# independently, synchronization NONE, automatic inheritance NONE.
#
# DATABASE-ONLY. No application file changes, so no application deploy occurs
# and the v309 deployment-ordering finding is not exercised by this release.
#
# ROLLBACK. The default changes are reversible by restoring the prior defaults.
# A column created by this release on a shape-B predecessor is reversible by
# dropping it. The row repair is NOT automatically reversible — the pre-image is
# the five production rows carrying the Burger Bar tenant UUID against an
# EventCore Demo parent, and it must be captured in the production preflight
# evidence before the migration runs.
release          v310.1
migration        supabase/v310_1_tenant_integrity.sql
migration_source repository
min_release      v310

function         v310_1_tenant_integrity()

# DECLARED POSTCONDITION. The marker function above is the whole of this
# release's declared deployment postcondition, and that is deliberate. The
# verifier's key vocabulary describes OBJECTS a release creates — function,
# table, view, index, trigger, policy, superseded — and v310.1 creates exactly
# one object. Its other effects are column defaults, a conditionally added
# column and a bounded row repair, none of which the deploy vocabulary can
# express. Inventing keys for them was rejected, correctly: an unrecognised key
# silently drops a requirement.
#
# Those effects are not left unverified. They are asserted by the release's own
# gates, which is the right place for them:
#   · four tenant_id columns present with the canonical tenants(id) FK — UT-4
#   · four defaults resolving through current_tenant_id()               — UT-2, TI-2
#   · zero hardcoded uuid tenant defaults anywhere in public            — UT-3, TI-3
#   · zero proposal_versions parent/child mismatches, no row gained or
#     lost by the repair                                                — UT-5, TI-6
#   · the repair converges a wrong row and spares a correct sibling     — TI-5
#   · RLS posture and policy count unchanged across the migration       — UT-7
